{:edb/db-before-t 2696 :edb/db-after-t 2697 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536009 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:51:53.761Z" 13194139536009 true] [17592186124533 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379243 13194139536009 true] [17592186124533 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379264 13194139536009 true] [17592186124566 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379250 13194139536009 true] [17592186124566 1020 #edb/ref 17592186379257 13194139536009 true] [17592186125180 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536009 true] [17592186125180 1100 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x, y, z) = x + 3y - z$ on the sphere $x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 11$." 13194139536009 false] [17592186125180 1100 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+3y-z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=11.$" 13194139536009 true] [17592186125180 1145 "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=11.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=11\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3=2\\lambda y \\\\ -1=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=11$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{11}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =11 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=\\pm 3,\\,z=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(1,3,-1),\\,(-1,-3,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,3,-1)$ | $(-1,-3,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $11$ | $-11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,-3,1),$ and its minimum value is $-11.$" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379243 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379244 13194139536009 true] [17592186379243 1089 "q-101310" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379243 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536009 true] [17592186379243 1100 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=3-y+5z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379243 1113 #uuid "83b46289-006f-5fe0-b0f6-c2019e283781" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379243 1145 "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=26\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,-1,5⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y \\\\ 5=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=26$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =26 \\\\ 0+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ \\frac{26}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=∓1\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(0,-1,5),\\,(0,1,-5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-1,5)\\,$ | $\\,(0,1,-5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,29\\,$ | $\\,-23\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,1,-5),$ and its minimum value is $-23.$" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379245 13194139536009 true] [17592186379244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379246 13194139536009 true] [17592186379244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379247 13194139536009 true] [17592186379244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379248 13194139536009 true] [17592186379244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379249 13194139536009 true] [17592186379244 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536009 true] [17592186379244 1041 "selection" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379244 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379249 13194139536009 true] [17592186379244 1110 #uuid "52cefdc7-90a7-5c36-8f10-002a6cd113cf" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379245 1034 "-40" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379245 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536009 true] [17592186379245 1111 #uuid "2f41d8e0-9dbc-5418-ba0e-c763a8290ecb" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379246 1034 "-14" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379246 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536009 true] [17592186379246 1111 #uuid "f5972f38-6314-5b9b-adb2-a2de311a9b84" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379247 1034 "-35" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379247 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536009 true] [17592186379247 1111 #uuid "3b9cafcb-58df-576b-9c08-131583771c13" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379248 1034 "-26" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379248 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536009 true] [17592186379248 1111 #uuid "12779d99-4202-566d-9cc0-4854cc58759c" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379249 1034 "-23" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379249 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536009 true] [17592186379249 1111 #uuid "6bb80e85-bf93-5673-b51e-cc95c396e584" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379250 1017 #edb/ref 17592186379251 13194139536009 true] [17592186379250 1089 "q-162763" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379250 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536009 true] [17592186379250 1100 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4y-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379250 1113 #uuid "e912a000-72e3-5446-81b8-633418a71a74" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379250 1145 "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,4-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4-2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\sqrt{5}),(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-2+1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(\\pm 1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-\\sqrt{5})$ | $(0,\\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 1,-2)$ and its minimum value is $-14.$" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379251 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379252 13194139536009 true] [17592186379251 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379253 13194139536009 true] [17592186379251 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379254 13194139536009 true] [17592186379251 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379255 13194139536009 true] [17592186379251 1000 #edb/ref 17592186379256 13194139536009 true] [17592186379251 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536009 true] [17592186379251 1041 "selection" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379251 1104 #edb/ref 17592186379253 13194139536009 true] [17592186379251 1110 #uuid "1a717293-313c-513f-b79d-334f7ad34d9a" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379252 1034 "-9" 13194139536009 true] [17592186379252 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536009 true] [